use os, std, json, dictionary

# apilib: builds the site's API library from the Helium library index and hand-written pages.
# describe: asks a model for one line per declaration. build: writes the static pages.

All text lives in one global grow-only buffer `TXT`. Struct `Str` is an offset and length into it. `keep(s)` copies text in and `txt(s)` views it. Every struct below stores text as `Str`, so arrays of these structs stay indexable.

Struct `Sym` is one library declaration: `ns` (the part of the full name before `::`, empty when there is none), `name` (the part after it), `kind` int (0 function, 1 type, 2 constant or global), `decl`, `file`, `line` int, `doc`, then `topic`, `level` and `desc` (empty until descriptions are loaded). Global `SYMS`, a list of `Sym`.

`load_syms(path)` fills `SYMS` from a tab-separated file with the columns full name, kind, declaration, file, line, doc. The doc column may be missing. Lines with fewer than five columns are skipped.

Struct `Mod` is one library module: `file`, `cat`, `blurb`, and `dev` bool. Global `MODS`, a list of `Mod`. `load_mods(data)` reads `<data>/modules.tsv` (columns file, category slug, blurb) and sets `dev` for files listed one per line in `<data>/devonly.txt`. `blurb_of(file)` returns a module's blurb, or an empty view.

`load_descs(data)` reads every file in the folder `data` whose name starts with `desc.` and ends with `.tsv`. Each line has the columns file, decl, topic, level, description. It sets `topic`, `level` and `desc` on every `SYMS` entry with that exact file and decl, using a dictionary from file + tab + decl to the first index of that pair plus a linear walk of later entries only if the pair repeats, so loading stays fast for 5,000 symbols and 5,000 lines. Later lines win.

`ask(model, sys_path, prompt, tag, out)` writes `prompt` to `/tmp/apilib-<tag>.prompt`, runs `claude -p --model <model> --setting-sources '' --strict-mcp-config --tools '' --no-session-persistence --output-format text --system-prompt "$(cat <sys_path>)" < prompt > /tmp/apilib-<tag>.reply 2>/dev/null` through `run_command` with the paths single-quoted, reads the reply into `out`, deletes both temp files and returns true when the command exited 0 and the reply is not empty.

`describe_chunk(lib, data, model, tag, ids, topics, want_topics)` asks for descriptions of the `SYMS` entries named by `ids`, all from one file, using the system prompt file `<data>/describe.txt`. The prompt has: the module's file and blurb; the line "Topics already used in this module:" followed by `topics` joined with ", "; the line "Aim for about <want_topics> topics for the whole module."; the line "Declarations:" then one line per id with its position in `ids`, a tab and its decl; the line "Source:" then that file's first 30 lines, a line `...`, and its lines from 3 before the first id's line up to 25 past the last id's line. Reply lines are split on tabs. A line counts only with four or more fields, where the first is a position in range and the last three are a non-empty topic, a level of `pub` or `int`, and a non-empty description. Each counted line appends file, decl, topic, level and description, tab-separated, as one line to `<data>/desc.<tag>.tsv` with `append_file`, sets the entry's `topic` `level` and `desc`, and adds a topic not yet in `topics`. It returns how many lines counted.

`cmd_describe(lib, data, model, shard, shards)` walks the distinct files of `SYMS` in first-seen order and handles only those whose position modulo `shards` equals `shard`. For each file it takes the entries with an empty `desc`, in the order they appear, and cuts them into chunks of at most 90 entries whose first and last lines are at most 900 source lines apart. `want_topics` is the file's total entry count divided by 25, clamped to 2..35. `topics` starts with the topics already set on that file's entries. Each chunk goes to `describe_chunk`, tag `<shard>`; when entries in the chunk are still missing a description it asks again with only those, at most two more times. It prints one progress line per chunk with the file, the chunk size and the running totals done and left.

`arg_val(name, fallback)` returns the value of the command-line argument `name=value`, else `fallback`.

`esc(out, s)` appends `s` to `out` with `&`, `<`, `>` and `"` turned into HTML entities.

`slug(out, s)` appends a URL-safe form of `s`: lowercase ASCII letters and digits kept, every other run of bytes becomes one `-`, with no `-` at either end.

`inline_md(out, s)` appends one line of Markdown as HTML: backtick spans become `<code>` with escaped contents and no further processing inside, `**x**` becomes `<strong>`, `[text](url)` becomes a link (a `https://` url also gets `target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer"`, and the text may itself hold backtick spans), and all other text is escaped.

Struct `Heading` holds `text`, `id` and `level` int. Global `HEADS`, a list of `Heading` collects the headings of the current page and is cleared by whoever starts a page. Struct `Hit` holds `name`, `kind`, `module`, `url` (a full site path with any `#anchor`), `desc` and `level`, as one search index row. Global `HITS`, a list of `Hit`.

`add_hit(name, kind, module, url, desc, level)` appends a row to `HITS`. When `kind` is `guide` and a row with the same name and module already exists, it merges instead: the row takes the new url when that is longer, and the new desc when its own is empty.

`render_md(out, md, page_url, page_title)` appends a Markdown page as HTML. It works line by line. A line of three or more backticks opens a fence that closes on a line starting with the same number of backticks, and its body becomes `<pre><code>` escaped. `#`, `##`, `###` lines become `h1`, `h2`, `h3` holding the text through `inline_md`. The `h2` and `h3` get an `id` made by `slug` of the text with backticks removed, are added to `HEADS`, and each adds a search row with name the text without backticks, kind `guide`, module `page_title`, url `page_url` + `#` + id, an empty desc and level `pub`. Runs of `- ` lines become `ul`, runs of `1. ` style lines become `ol`, a run of lines starting with `|` becomes a table inside `<div class="lib-tw">` with the first row as `th` cells and the separator row dropped, cells split on unescaped `|` outside backticks and trimmed. Each body row whose first cell starts with a backtick adds a search row with name the first code span of that cell, kind `guide`, module `page_title`, url `page_url` followed by `#` and the id of the latest `h2` or `h3`, or `page_url` alone with no `#` when the page has no such heading yet, desc the row's last cell with Markdown marks removed, and level `pub`. A blank line ends a paragraph; other consecutive lines join into one `<p>` run through `inline_md`.

`include_md(out, md, gold_dir)` copies `md` to `out` with each line of the form `@include path` replaced by the contents of `<gold_dir>/path`.

`mod_slug(out, file)` appends the file path without its extension and with `/` turned into `-`. `mod_name(out, file)` appends the path without its extension. `mod_import(out, file)` appends the path without its extension and with `/` turned into `.`.

Struct `Page` is one guide page: `slug`, `title`, `url`, `group` int (0 Gold, 1 Helium language), `md` (its Markdown). Global `PAGES`, a list of `Page`. `load_pages(content, gold_dir)` fills it. For the group folders `gold` and `helium` under `content` it lists the `.md` files sorted by name, skipping `language.md`. A file name is two digits, a dash, then the slug. The title is the file's first `# ` line. Slugs `start` and `overview` get the url `/api-library/gold/` and `/api-library/helium/`. Other Gold pages get `/api-library/gold/<slug>`, other Helium pages get `/api-library/helium/language/<slug>`. Includes are expanded with `include_md`. Then `content/helium/language.md` is split at each `## ` line into one more Helium page per section: the title is the heading with backticks removed, the slug is `slug` of that title, the url is `/api-library/helium/language/<slug>`, and the md is `# ` + the heading with its backticks kept, followed by the section's lines. Before the split the special title `Standard library (std is implicit)` becomes `The standard library at a glance`, `Minimal by construction (std constructs and waste warnings)` becomes `Compact constructs and warnings`, `Core` becomes `Core language` and `Web` becomes `Web builds`.

Struct `Cat` holds `slug`, `title`, `blurb`. Global `CATS`, a list of `Cat`. `load_cats(data)` reads `<data>/categories.tsv` (columns slug, title, blurb).

`count_of(file, kind)` counts `SYMS` entries of a file and kind, with `file` empty meaning all files. `cat_count(cat)` counts the `SYMS` entries in the modules of a category.

`load_page_descs(data)` reads `<data>/pagedesc.tsv` (columns url, description) into a dictionary. `page_desc(url)` returns a guide page's description and aborts naming the url when it has none.

`side_html(out, current, current_cat, sub)` appends the sidebar. First `<h2>Helium library</h2>` and a `ul` of the categories, each linking to `/api-library/helium/<cat slug>` with its symbol count in a `<span>` inside the `<a>`. When a category's slug equals `current_cat`, its `li` also holds a nested `ul` of its modules in `modules.tsv` order, linking to `/api-library/helium/lib/<mod_slug>` and named by `mod_name`. Then two groups, `<details class="lib-g"><summary>Gold</summary>` with a `ul` of the Gold pages and `<details class="lib-g"><summary>Helium language</summary>` with a `ul` of the Helium pages. A group's `details` has the `open` attribute when `current` is the url of one of its pages or is `/api-library/`. A link whose url equals `current` gets `class="on"`, and `sub`, which is already HTML and may be empty, comes right after that link inside the same `li`. Titles are escaped.

`fill_page(site, data, rel, title, desc, url, crumbs, main, side, ver, date)` loads `<data>/page.html`, replaces `{{title}}` `{{desc}}` `{{url}}` `{{crumbs}}` `{{main}}` `{{side}}` `{{ver}}` and `{{date}}`, replaces `{{nav}}` with the trimmed contents of `<site>/tools/nav.html`, and writes the result to `<site>/api-library/<rel>` creating folders with `mkdir -p`. `desc` and `title` are escaped first and `desc` has its backticks removed. The other values are already HTML. Replacement is one pass over the template, so text inside `main` is never scanned for placeholders.

`crumb(out, url, text)` appends ` <span>&rsaquo;</span> ` when `out` is not empty, then a link to `url` with the escaped text, or just the escaped text when `url` is empty. The first crumb of every page is `API Library` linking to `/api-library/`.

`write_guide_pages(site, data, ver, date)`, all text, writes every page of `PAGES`. The file is `gold/index.html` or `helium/index.html` for the two index urls, `gold/<slug>.html` for Gold pages and `helium/language/<slug>.html` for the rest. The description is `page_desc` of its url. The sidebar's `sub` is `<ul class="lib-sub">` with one link per level 2 entry of `HEADS` after rendering the page, `<a href="#id">text</a>` with the text escaped, or empty when there are none. The Helium overview page also appends after its Markdown `<h2 id="library">Standard library</h2>` and the category cards from `cat_cards`. At the bottom each page gets `<nav class="lib-pn">` linking the previous and next page of the same group as `<a class="prev" href="url">&larr; title</a>` and `<a class="next" href="url">title &rarr;</a>` with titles escaped, and `<span></span>` in place of a missing one. Crumbs are API Library, then `Gold` or `Helium` linking to its index url, then the page title; on the two index pages they end at `Gold` or `Helium`, unlinked. Each page also adds one search row with name its title, kind `guide`, module `Gold` or `Helium`, url its url, desc its description and level `pub`.

`cat_cards(out)` appends `<div class="lib-grid">` with one `<a class="lib-card">` per category, linking to `/api-library/helium/<slug>`: `<strong>` the title, `<span>` the blurb, `<small>` the symbol count plus " functions and types".

`struct_body(out, lib, file, line, decl)` appends the full source text of a type declaration starting at 1-based `line` in `<lib>/<file>`. If that line has a `{`, it takes lines until the braces balance. Otherwise it takes the line and any following lines indented deeper than it. It stops at 60 lines, adding a last line `...`. Trailing `;` is dropped. When the file cannot be read or the line does not contain the declared type's name it appends `decl`. Source files are cached in a dictionary from file to an offset in `TXT` so each is read once, and its lines are split only when needed.

`clean_sig(out, decl)` appends a declaration with a leading `extern ` or `fn ` removed, and a trailing `;` removed, trimmed.

`file_notes(out, lib, file)` appends the comment block at the very top of a source file as plain text with the comment marks (`//` or `#`) and one following space removed: every line from the first until the first line that is not a comment. It appends nothing when the file does not begin with a comment.

`sym_card(out, lib, sha, dev, ids, anchor)` appends one `<article class="sym" id="<anchor>">` for the `SYMS` entries in `ids`, which share a name. The heading is `<h3><a href="#<anchor>">name</a> <i>function|type|constant</i></h3>` with `<i>` also saying `, N forms` when there are several ids. Each id then adds a `<pre>` with the escaped signature (`struct_body` for kind 1, `clean_sig` otherwise) and a `<p>` with the escaped `desc` when it is not empty and not the same as the previous id's. The first id's `doc` adds `<p class="note">` with the escaped doc, after dropping any leading run of `-` and spaces, unless it is the same as `desc` or has fewer than four words, because a shorter doc is only a section label from the source. The card ends with the location of the first id as `<a class="src" href="https://github.com/Amnibro/helium/blob/<sha>/hc/lib/<file>#L<line>">hc/lib/<file>:<line></a>`, or a plain `<span class="src">` with the same text when `dev`.

`write_module(site, data, lib, sha, ver, date, m)`, where `m` is a module and the rest are text, writes `helium/lib/<mod_slug>.html`. The h1 is `mod_name`. Next comes `<p class="lib-lede">` with the blurb through `inline_md`. When `dev`, a `<div class="lib-dev">` says "Coming soon: this module is not in the public Helium repository yet, so importing it from a public checkout will fail." A `<p class="lib-meta">` shows the import: for `std.he` the words "Always available, no import needed", otherwise `<code>import <mod_import>;</code>`, then the most common non-empty `ns` as `<code>ns::</code>` when any entry has one, then the counts written like "18 functions, 1 types, 4 constants", and a "Source" link to the file on GitHub at `sha` unless `dev`. File notes of three lines or more go in `<details class="lib-notes"><summary>Notes from the source</summary><pre>`. Entries of the module are grouped by exact name into cards. A card with any entry of level other than `int` is public; the rest are internal. Public cards are grouped by the `topic` of their first entry, with an empty topic shown as `More`. Topics keep the order in which they first appear in the source, except that a topic's cards keep source order. `<ul class="lib-pills">` lists each topic as `<a href="#<topic slug>">topic <span class="n">count</span></a>`, followed by a link to `#internal` named "Internal helpers" with no count when there are internal cards. Each topic becomes `<h2 id="<topic slug>">topic <span class="n">count</span></h2>` and its cards. Internal cards go at the end in `<details class="lib-int" id="internal"><summary>Internal helpers (count)</summary>`. Anchors are the name, with `-2`, `-3` and so on when a name was already used as an anchor on the page, and never equal to a topic's slug or `internal`, since those get a `-2` as well. After writing the page, for every entry in `SYMS` order, it adds a search row: name the entry's `ns::name` when `ns` is set, otherwise `name`; kind function, type or constant; module `mod_name`; url the module page + `#` + anchor; desc; level. A closing `<p class="note">` says "Descriptions and topics were drafted by AI from the source code. Signatures, types and source links come straight from the code." Crumbs are API Library, Helium, the category, then the module name. The sidebar has the category expanded and the module as `current`, and its `sub` is `<ul class="lib-sub">` with one `<a href="#<topic slug>">topic</a>` per topic, then `<a href="#internal">Internal helpers</a>` when there are internal cards.

`write_category(site, data, ver, date, c)`, where `c` is a category and the rest are text, writes `helium/<slug>.html`: the h1 title, `<p class="lib-lede">` blurb, then `<div class="lib-grid">` with one `<a class="lib-card">` per module of the category in `modules.tsv` order: `<strong>` `mod_name`, `<span>` the blurb through `inline_md` but with its code spans shown as plain text inside `<code>`, `<small>` the counts of functions, types and constants, and `<em class="lib-tag">Coming soon</em>` when `dev`. Crumbs are API Library, Helium, the title.

`write_landing(site, data, ver, date)`, all text, writes `index.html`, titled `API Library`, described as "Every Gold command and every Helium function, type and constant, grouped by what it does and searchable." It holds the h1, a lede with that description, `<h2>Helium library</h2>` with the sentence "<symbol count> functions, types and constants in <module count> modules." and `cat_cards`, `<h2>Gold</h2>` with one card per Gold page in a `<div class="lib-grid">`, and `<h2>Helium language</h2>` with one card per Helium page in another `<div class="lib-grid">`. A guide card is an `<a class="lib-card">` with `<strong>` the title and `<span>` the `page_desc` of its url. It ends with `<h2>For AI agents</h2>` and the paragraph "The full library as JSON: " then a link to `/api-library/index.json` showing that path, then ".".

`write_search(site)` writes `<site>/api-library/search.tsv`, one line per `HITS` row with the columns name, kind, module, url, desc, level, with tabs and newlines in a value replaced by spaces.

`write_json(site, lib, sha)`, all text, writes `<site>/api-library/index.json`: an object with `source` (`https://github.com/Amnibro/helium`), `commit` (the sha) and `modules`, an array of objects with `file`, `import`, `category`, `public` (false when `dev`), `summary` and `symbols`, an array of objects with `name` (bare, with no `ns::`), `kind` (`function`, `type` or `constant`), `signature`, `topic`, `level`, `description`, `line`. Strings are JSON-escaped. It uses `json` library writers when they fit.

`cmd_build(lib, data, content, site, gold_dir, sha, ver, date, hc, hc_lib, search)`, all text, loads the categories, page descriptions and pages, writes the guide pages, every category, every module and the landing page, then the search index and the JSON, and copies `<data>/lib.css` to `<site>/api-library/lib.css`. It then compiles the search box by running `HC_LIB=<hc_lib> <hc> build --web <search> -o <site>/api-library/search.wasm` through `run_command` with each value single-quoted, and aborts when that fails. It prints how many pages and search rows it wrote. Modules are written after guide pages so the search rows put guides first. Every category in `CATS` and every module in `MODS` must be written, and a module whose category is unknown aborts with a message.

`arg_val(name, fallback)` returns the value of the command-line argument `name=value`, else `fallback`.

`capture(cmd, out)` clears `out`, runs `cmd` through `run_command` with stdout and stderr redirected to `/tmp/apilib-capture.txt`, reads that file into `out`, deletes it and returns true when the exit code was 0. `shq(out, s)` appends `s` single-quoted for the shell, with embedded single quotes handled. `read_state(state, name, out)` loads `<state>/<name>` trimmed into `out`, leaving it empty when the file is missing, and `write_state(state, name, value)` writes it.

`ensure_modules(lib, data, model)` makes every library file appear in `MODS`. For each distinct file of `SYMS` with no `MODS` row it asks the model with system prompt file `<data>/module.txt`, tag `mod`, and a prompt holding the category slugs with their titles, then the file's path and its first 70 lines. The first reply line is split on a tab into a slug and a one-sentence summary. When the slug is not a category it takes the category of the first `MODS` row in the same top folder, else `logic`. An empty summary becomes `The <mod_name> library module.`. The row is appended to `MODS` and as a line to `<data>/modules.tsv`. Afterward it removes from `MODS`, but not from the file, every module with no `SYMS` entries, so a deleted source file loses its page.

`refresh_language(helium, content)` writes `<content>/helium/language.md` from `<helium>/HELIUM.md`, keeping the lines from the first `## ` line to the end.

`refresh_guide(data, model, path, source)` asks the model to update one guide page, with system prompt file `<data>/guide.txt`, tag `guide`, and a prompt of `Current page:`, the text of `path`, then `Current help text and source:`, then `source`. It removes outer blank lines from the reply and writes it to `path` only when its first line equals the page's first line and it is at least half the page's length.

`refresh_guides(helium, gold, content, data, model, state)` keeps the command pages in step. The Helium source text is the output of `<helium>/build/hcn help` plus every line of `<helium>/hc/hc.he` containing "m[..] == \"-". The Gold source text is the output of `<gold>/gold` with no arguments plus every line of `git -C <gold> show HEAD:gold.amni` containing `envor("GOLD_` or `env("GOLD_`. Each is compared with the state file `helium-help` or `gold-help`. When the file is missing it is only written. When the text differs, Helium refreshes `<content>/helium/10-commands.md`, Gold refreshes `<content>/gold/10-commands.md` and `<content>/gold/30-settings.md`, then the state file is written.

`publish_site(site, sha, old_rows)` finishes a site build in the worktree `site`. It returns false, after restoring the folder with `git -C <site> checkout -- api-library` and `git -C <site> clean -fdq api-library`, when `HITS.len` is below four fifths of `old_rows`. It runs `node tools/nav.js` and `node tools/build-sitemap.js` inside `site`, stages `api-library` and `sitemap.xml`, restores every other change with `git -C <site> checkout -- .`, and returns true when nothing is staged. Otherwise it commits with the message `API library: update to Helium <first 7 of sha>` and runs `git -C <site> push -q origin HEAD:main`. If the push fails it runs `git -C <site> pull -q --rebase origin main` and pushes once more, returning whether that worked.

A line is shaped like a credential when one of `ghp_`, `gho_`, `github_pat_`, `sk-ant-`, `AKIA`, `AIza`, `xoxb-`, `AMNIPRO` is followed directly by at least 16 characters that are ASCII letters, digits, `_` or `-`, or when the line holds a PEM private key header: five dashes then the word BEGIN and a space, and later on that line the words PRIVATE KEY then five dashes. Search for those two pieces in separate statements. This looks at the shape so that a program listing these prefixes does not match itself.

`publish_gold(gold, public_dir, state, date)` refreshes the public Gold repository at most once per day, doing nothing when state `public-date` equals `date`. When `os::is_dir(<public_dir>/.git)` is false it clones `https://github.com/Amnibro/gold.git` there, printing `public clone failed` and returning when that fails. It runs `git -C <public_dir> pull -q --ff-only`, adds a temporary worktree of `gold` at HEAD with `git -C <gold> worktree add -q --detach <state>/gold-export HEAD`, printing `could not add the export worktree` and returning when that fails, runs `<state>/gold-export/tools/public.sh <public_dir>`, removes the worktree with `git -C <gold> worktree remove --force <state>/gold-export`, restores the generated phrase library with `git -C <public_dir> checkout -q HEAD -- lib` and `git -C <public_dir> clean -fdq lib`, because it changes hourly and stays out of this refresh, copies `<gold>/tools/apilib/data/api.tsv` over `<public_dir>/packs/std/api.tsv` so the public index matches public Helium main, and runs `git -C <public_dir> add -A`. With nothing staged it just records the date. Otherwise it refuses to publish, printing why, unstaging with `git -C <public_dir> reset -q --hard HEAD`, and leaving the date unrecorded, when `git -C <public_dir> diff --cached` has an added line shaped like a credential, or when a staged path is not under one of `README.md`, `GOLD.md`, `gold`, `gold.amni`, `goldc.amni`, `.gitignore`, `dialects`, `primers`, `spec`, `runtime`, `examples`, `tools`, `tests`, `packs/std`, `demos`, `bench`, `codex`. It then commits with the message `Update Gold to <date>` using `-c user.name` and `-c user.email` taken from `git -C <gold> log -1 --format=%an` and `%ae`, printing `commit to the public Gold repository failed` and returning when the commit fails, pushes with `git -C <public_dir> push -q origin HEAD`, and records the date when the push worked.

`cmd_sync(helium, gold, site, public_dir, state, data, content, model)`, all text, returns an exit code and brings the whole library up to date. In order:
1. It runs `mkdir -p <state>`. When `<state>/pause` exists it prints `paused` and returns 0.
2. When `git -C <gold> status --porcelain -- tools/apilib dialects` prints anything it prints that it is skipping because the files are being edited, and returns 0.
3. It runs `git -C <helium> fetch -q helium main` and reads `sha` from `git -C <helium> rev-parse helium/main`. When state `helium-sha` differs it runs `git -C <helium> checkout -q -f --detach <sha>`, since this checkout belongs to the sync and the bootstrap rewrites a tracked seed file, builds with `BUILD=<helium>/build.new <helium>/hc/bootstrap.sh`, then swaps by removing `<helium>/build.old`, renaming `build` to `build.old` and `build.new` to `build`, and records `helium-sha`. A failed bootstrap returns 1 and leaves `build` alone.
4. `key` is `sha`, a space, `git -C <gold> rev-parse HEAD:tools/apilib`, a space, and `git -C <gold> rev-parse HEAD:dialects`. `date` comes from `date +%F`. When state `key` equals `key` it runs `publish_gold` and returns 0.
5. It runs `<gold>/gold pack apilib <helium>/hc/lib lang=he 'skip=^(rt|lutgpu|physics_engine)\.he$'` and returns 1 when that fails. It copies `<gold>/packs/apilib/api.tsv` to `<data>/api.tsv` and runs `rm -rf <gold>/packs/apilib`, so Gold's own `std` pack is never touched. It loads `<data>/api.tsv` with `load_syms`, then the categories, the modules and the descriptions. `lib` is `<helium>/hc/lib`. It runs `ensure_modules`, then `cmd_describe(lib, data, model, 0, 1)`, then `refresh_language` and `refresh_guides`.
6. It reloads nothing further and prepares the site: `git -C <site> fetch -q origin main`, `git -C <site> checkout -q --detach origin/main`, `git -C <site> checkout -- .`, counts the lines of `<site>/api-library/search.tsv` as `old_rows`, and runs `rm -rf <site>/api-library`. `ver` is the first 7 bytes of `sha` plus the first 3 bytes of the `tools/apilib` tree id. It calls `cmd_build(lib, data, content, site, gold, sha, ver, date, <helium>/build/hcn, lib, search.amni)`, then `publish_site`, returning 1 when that is false.
7. It runs `git -C <gold> add -- tools/apilib`. When `git -C <gold> diff --cached --quiet -- tools/apilib` reports changes it commits only that path with `git -C <gold> commit -q -m "API library: update to Helium <first 7 of sha>" -- tools/apilib`, and runs `git -C <gold> push -q amni HEAD`.
8. It runs `publish_gold`. State `tries` counts the runs in a row that ended with a `SYMS` entry lacking a `desc`. When there is none, or when `tries` reaches 3, it prints how many are missing, resets `tries` to 0, recomputes `key`, since step 7 can change the tree, and records it. Otherwise it stores the higher count, so the entries the model missed get tried again on the next run without retrying forever. It returns 0.

`main()` looks at the first argument. `data` comes from `data=` (default `data`), `gold` from `gold=` (default `../..`) and `model` from `model=` (default `sonnet`). For `describe` and `build` it first loads `<data>/api.tsv` with `load_syms`, the modules and the existing descriptions. `describe` runs `cmd_describe` with the arguments `lib=`, `shard=` (default 0) and `shards=` (default 1), parsed as integers where needed. `build` runs `cmd_build` with `lib=`, `content=` (default `content`), `site=`, `sha=`, `ver=`, `date=`, `hc=`, `hclib=` and `search=` (default `search.amni`). `sync` exits with the result of `cmd_sync` using `helium=`, `site=`, `public=`, `state=` and `content=` (default `content`). Anything else prints a usage line and exits with 2.
